{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1786613299330", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-8246e280ab1c19a4", "name": "Bash", "input": {"command": "python3 -c \"\nimport re\nwith open('/tmp/paper_2608.11898.html', 'r') as f:\n    html = f.read()\n# Extract abstract\nm = re.search(r'<blockquote[^>]*class=\\\"abstract[^>]*\\\">(.*?)</blockquote>', html, re.DOTALL)\nif m:\n    text = re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', '', m.group(1)).strip()\n    print('ABSTRACT:')\n    print(text[:2000])\nprint('---')\n# Extract title\nm2 = re.search(r'<h1[^>]*class=\\\"title[^>]*\\\">(.*?)</h1>', html, re.DOTALL)\nif m2:\n    print('TITLE:', re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', '', m2.group(1)).strip())\n# Extract subjects\nm3 = re.search(r'<td[^>]*class=\\\"tablecell subjects[^>]*\\\">(.*?)</td>', html, re.DOTALL)\nif m3:\n    print('SUBJECTS:', re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', '', m3.group(1)).strip())\n\" 2>&1", "description": "Extract abstract and metadata from HTML"}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8465b4bb-521f-4630-bf50-65701cfd10f7", "uuid": "f0d9bcfb-f15b-4f67-8910-1be7fe7d171b"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-baf3d7ecd44781bd", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# Qwen3-ASR Technical Report\narXiv: 2601.21337\nAuthors: Xian Shi, Xiong Wang, Zhifang Guo, Yongqi Wang, Pei Zhang, Xinyu Zhang, Zishan Guo, Hongkun Hao, Yu Xi, Baosong Yang, Jin Xu, Jingren Zhou, Junyang Lin\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: asr\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces the Qwen3-ASR family, featuring two all-in-one ASR models that achieve state-of-the-art multilingual and dialectal speech recognition while remaining competitive with top proprietary APIs.\n  2. Presents Qwen3-ForcedAligner-0.6B, the first LLM-based non-autoregressive forced aligner that provides highly accurate multilingual timestamps at flexible granularities.\n  3. Demonstrates exceptional inference efficiency, with the Qwen3-ASR-0.6B model achieving an optimal accuracy-efficiency trade-off and processing up to 2,000 seconds of audio per second at high concurrency.\n  4. Releases all model weights under the Apache 2.0 license alongside a comprehensive, open-source inference and fine-tuning framework to accelerate speech recognition research.\n\nTags: ASR, ASR/隐式对齐-AED, ASR/训练-监督", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8465b4bb-521f-4630-bf50-65701cfd10f7", "uuid": "f70b9c39-4648-471d-8ef9-d3a1bf24bc01", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T09:28:27.079Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# Qwen3-ASR Technical Report\narXiv: 2601.21337\nAuthors: Xian Shi, Xiong Wang, Zhifang Guo, Yongqi Wang, Pei Zhang, Xinyu Zhang, Zishan Guo, Hongkun Hao, Yu Xi, Baosong Yang, Jin Xu, Jingren Zhou, Junyang Lin\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: asr\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces the Qwen3-ASR family, featuring two all-in-one ASR models that achieve state-of-the-art multilingual and dialectal speech recognition while remaining competitive with top proprietary APIs.\n  2. Presents Qwen3-ForcedAligner-0.6B, the first LLM-based non-autoregressive forced aligner that provides highly accurate multilingual timestamps at flexible granularities.\n  3. Demonstrates exceptional inference efficiency, with the Qwen3-ASR-0.6B model achieving an optimal accuracy-efficiency trade-off and processing up to 2,000 seconds of audio per second at high concurrency.\n  4. Releases all model weights under the Apache 2.0 license alongside a comprehensive, open-source inference and fine-tuning framework to accelerate speech recognition research.\n\nTags: ASR, ASR/隐式对齐-AED, ASR/训练-监督", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8246e280ab1c19a4", "type": "tool_result", "content": "ABSTRACT:\nAbstract:Recent large-scale ASR models already achieve strong Mandarin recognition accuracy and have some ability to recognize Chinese dialects. However, their dialect recognition accuracy is still limited in real-world speech. Direct dialect adaptation can lower dialect CER, but it may also raise Mandarin CER. We therefore study how to adapt a capable ASR model to improve multi-dialect recognition without degrading Mandarin recognition. We adopt an adaptation pipeline where continual pre-training (CPT) and dialect supervised fine-tuning (SFT) provide a strong foundation, and On-Policy Self-Distillation (OPSD) serves as the final refinement. OPSD addresses the train--test mismatch in autoregressive ASR by training the student model on its own decoded prefixes while a frozen teacher, conditioned on the reference transcript as privileged context, provides soft token-level targets. This replaces hard cross-entropy updates on dialect data with distillation, preserving Mandarin ability while refining dialect recognition. We instantiate the framework with Qwen3-ASR-1.7B and evaluate it on public and internal Mandarin and dialect test sets. Under matched refinement data and schedule, OPSD improves dialect recognition without raising Mandarin CER, whereas continued teacher-forced fine-tuning increases Mandarin CER. We will release the model weights and evaluation scripts.\n---\nTITLE: Title:On-Policy Self-Distillation for Multi-Dialect ASR: Mastering Dialects, Retaining Mandarin\nSUBJECTS: Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "8465b4bb-521f-4630-bf50-65701cfd10f7", "uuid": "1ce0a621-d366-4f7b-9335-4b1a5b2bb179", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T09:28:27.196Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "ABSTRACT:\nAbstract:Recent large-scale ASR models already achieve strong Mandarin recognition accuracy and have some ability to recognize Chinese dialects. However, their dialect recognition accuracy is still limited in real-world speech. Direct dialect adaptation can lower dialect CER, but it may also raise Mandarin CER. We therefore study how to adapt a capable ASR model to improve multi-dialect recognition without degrading Mandarin recognition. We adopt an adaptation pipeline where continual pre-training (CPT) and dialect supervised fine-tuning (SFT) provide a strong foundation, and On-Policy Self-Distillation (OPSD) serves as the final refinement. OPSD addresses the train--test mismatch in autoregressive ASR by training the student model on its own decoded prefixes while a frozen teacher, conditioned on the reference transcript as privileged context, provides soft token-level targets. This replaces hard cross-entropy updates on dialect data with distillation, preserving Mandarin ability while refining dialect recognition. We instantiate the framework with Qwen3-ASR-1.7B and evaluate it on public and internal Mandarin and dialect test sets. Under matched refinement data and schedule, OPSD improves dialect recognition without raising Mandarin CER, whereas continued teacher-forced fine-tuning increases Mandarin CER. We will release the model weights and evaluation scripts.\n---\nTITLE: Title:On-Policy Self-Distillation for Multi-Dialect ASR: Mastering Dialects, Retaining Mandarin\nSUBJECTS: Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
