 Suzhou falls from\n50.35% to 26.73%. OPSD then adds further gains where early errors are more\nlikely to accumulate: Nanchang from 18.63% to 15.58%, Kejia from 32.01% to\n28.60%, and Chaoshan from 27.34% to 25.21%. A few easier sets such as Shang-\nhai and Sichuan are slightly better under SFT than OPSD, so the refinement\n\n=== PAGE 12 ===\n12\nS. Wang et al.\nTable 7. CER (%) on the ablation of CPT before dialect SFT. Lower is better; bold\nmarks the best.\nModel\nCPT SFT Mandarin Dialect Internal Overall\nAvg.\nAvg.\nAvg.\nAvg.\nQwen3-ASR\n–\n–\n3.46\n15.37\n21.01\n15.57\nw/o CPT\n–\n✓\n5.16\n14.70\n14.20\n11.95\nw/ CPT\n✓\n✓\n3.40\n13.16\n13.30\n10.72\nTable 8. CER (%) on the final refinement step. Continued SFT and OPSD start from\nthe same SFT checkpoint. Lower is better; bold marks the best.\nModel\nMandarin\nDialect\nInternal\nOverall\nAvg.\nAvg.\nAvg.\nAvg.\nSFT checkpoint\n3.40\n13.16\n13.30\n10.72\n+ Continued SFT\n4.43\n12.89\n12.95\n10.74\n+ OPSD\n3.27\n12.79\n12.42\n10.12\nis not uniformly best on every dialect. Overall, the pattern supports CPT for\nbuilding dialect coverage and OPSD as a final refinement on difficult varieties.\n6\nAblation Studies\nWe use two ablations to check the main claims. The first asks whether CPT\nis useful before dialect SFT. The second asks whether OPSD is better than\ncontinuing cross-entropy training on the same data. Tables 7 and 8 report the\ntwo comparisons separately.\n6.1\nEffect of CPT\nTable 7 compares the Qwen3-ASR, dialect SFT without CPT, and dialect SFT\nafter CPT. Direct dialect SFT lowers Dialect Avg. CER from 15.37% to 14.70%\nand Internal Avg. CER from 21.01% to 14.20%, but substantially raises Man-\ndarin Avg. CER from 3.46% to 5.16%. Overall Avg. CER falls from 15.57% to\n11.95% under the macro-average, yet this is not a balanced gain because Man-\ndarin Avg. CER increases substantially. Adding CPT before SFT yields 3.40%\nMandarin Avg. CER, 13.16% Dialect Avg. CER, 13.30% Internal Avg. CER, and\n10.72% Overall Avg. CER. Thus CPT on Mandarin-dialect data before dialect\nSFT is needed for a stable Mandarin–dialect trade-off and provides the SFT\ncheckpoint used by OPSD.\n6.2\nEffect of OPSD\nTable 8 starts from the same SFT checkpoint. Continued SFT slightly lowers\nDialect Avg. CER from 13.16% to 12.89% and Internal Avg. CER from 13.30% to\n\n=== PAGE 13 ===\nOPSD for Multi-Dialect ASR\n13\n12.95%, but raises Mandarin Avg. CER from 3.40% to 4.43%; Overall Avg. CER\nrises from 10.72% to 10.74%. OPSD lowers all three group averages and Overall\nAvg. CER to 10.12%. Both runs use the same Dref and schedule (Section 4.4), so\nthe difference is the objective rather than extra data or training budget. Relative\nto Continued SFT, OPSD lowers Mandarin Avg. CER from 4.43% to 3.27%,\nDialect Avg. CER from 12.89% to 12.79%, Internal Avg. CER from 12.95% to\n12.42%, and Overall Avg. CER from 10.74% to 10.12%.\n7\nConclusion\nReferences\n1. Agarwal, R., Vieillard, N., Zhou, Y., Stanczyk, P., Ramos, S., et al.: On-policy\ndistillation of language models: Learning from self-generated mistakes. In: Proc.\nICLR (2024)\n2. Ahmad, R., Farooq, M.U., Hain, T.: Progressive unsupervised domain adaptation\nfor ASR using ensemble models and multi-stage training. In: Proc. ICASSP. pp.\n11466–11470 (2024)\n3. Ardila, R., Branson, M., Davis, K., Henretty, M., Kohler, M., et al.: Common voice:\nA massively-multilingual speech corpus. In: Proc. LREC. pp. 4218–4222 (2020)\n4. Bai, J., Li, B., Li, Q., Sainath, T.N., Strohman, T.: Efficient adapter finetuning for\ntail languages in streaming multilingual ASR. In: Proc. ICASSP. pp. 10841–10845\n(2024)\n5. Bai, Y., Chen, J., Chen, J., Chen, W., Chen, Z., et al.: Seed-asr: Understand-\ning diverse speech and contexts with llm-based speech recognition. arXiv preprint\narXiv:2407.04675 (2024)\n6. Bengio, S., Vinyals, O., Jaitly, N., Shazeer, N.: Scheduled sampling for sequence\nprediction with recurrent neural networks. In: Proc. NeurIPS (2015)\n7. Bu, H., Du, J., Na, X., Wu, B., Zheng, H.: AISHELL-1: an open-source mandarin\nspeech corpus and a speech recognition baseline. In: Proc. O-COCOSDA. pp. 1–5\n(2017)\n8. Chang, H., Lee, H., Lee, L.: Towards lifelong learning of end-to-end ASR. In: Proc.\nInterspeech. pp. 2551–2555 (2021)\n9. Chang, K., Shao, Y., Li, J., Yu, D.: Towards comprehensive semantic speech em-\nbeddings for chinese dialects. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.07274 (2026)\n10. Chu, Y., Xu, J., Zhou, X., Yang, Q., Zhang, S., et al.: Qwen-audio: Advancing\nuniversal audio understanding via unified large-scale audio-language models. arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2311.07919 (2023)\n11. Dai, Y., Zhang, Z., Wang, S., Li, L., Guo, Z., et al.: Wenetspeech-chuan: A large-\nscale sichuanese corpus with rich annotation for dialectal speech processing. arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2509.18004 (2025)\n12. Du, J., Na, X., Liu, X., Bu, H.: AISHELL-2: transforming mandarin ASR research\ninto industrial scale. arXiv preprint arXiv:1808.10583 (2018)\n13. Gao, Z., Li, Z., Wang, J., Luo, H., Shi, X., et al.: Funasr: A fundamental end-to-end\nspeech recognition toolkit. In: Proc. Interspeech. pp. 1593–1597 (2023)\n14. Higuchi, Y., Moritz, N., Roux, J.L., Hori, T.: Momentum pseudo-labeling for semi-\nsupervised speech recognition. In: Proc. Interspeech. pp. 726–730 (2021)\n15. Hinsvark, A., Delworth, N., Rio, M.D., McNamara, Q., Dong, J., et al.: Accented\nspeech \n=== PAGE 14 ===\n14\nS. Wang et al.\n16. Hu, E.J., Shen, Y., Wallis, P., Allen-Zhu, Z., Li, Y., et al.: LoRA: low-rank adap-\ntation of large language models. In: Proc. ICLR (2022)\n17. Hu, Y., Chen, C., Yang, C., Qin, C., Chen, P., et al.: Self-taught recognizer: Toward\nunsupervised adaptation for speech foundation models. In: Proc. NeurIPS (2024)\n18. Li, L., Guo, Z., Chen, H., Dai, Y., Zhang, Z., et al.: Wenetspeech-yue: A large-scale\ncantonese speech corpus with multi-dimensional annotation. In: Proc. AAAI. pp.\n31627–31635 (2026)\n19. Meng, Y., Zhong, H., Lin, G., Wang, G.